{
 "game": "game1.jsonl#0#7020000",
 "summary": "The run found Baron (an eternal xMult) at ante 1 but took Raised Fist as a rental the same ante and kept it until ante 5. It never held the $25 bank after ante 2: it went from $47 down to $25 on rerolls that found nothing, then from $34 to $10 in ante 3 and from $17 to $0 in ante 4. It sold its only x3 joker (Flower Pot) for Mime and split its planets between Pair and Two Pair, so it reached ante 5 with Pair at level 2 and Two Pair at level 3. Entering The Tooth at $0 with a rental put it at -$12, so it had no shop at ante 5 and died to the 37,500 Big Blind at 17%. What decided it was the slow money drain from rentals and overspending, and the lost xMult.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "A rental taken for survival is a temporary loan. As soon as the next blind is 90%+ safe without it, or any non-rental scoring joker shows up, sell the rental first, before any other joker. Never take a second rental while carrying one, and never take a rental that is also perishable or eternal.",
   "evidence": "Decision 7 took Raised Fist [rental] at ante 1. From decision 8 the outlook was 100% safe through the ante 2 boss, yet it was kept until decision 62. Decision 26 added Mystic Summit [perishable+rental] on top of it. At decision 58 the run sold Shoot the Moon, a non-rental, rather than Raised Fist. Decision 52 itself notes '$1 interest now and paying $3 a round for the rental'.",
   "cost": "About $3 x 12 rounds = roughly $36 in rent, plus interest tiers it kept knocking out. This alone explains most of the gap between $47 at ante 2 and -$12 at ante 5."
  },
  {
   "rule": "From ante 3, do not drop below $25 on packs or rerolls unless the outlook says the next blind or the current boss is below 70%. A weak outlook for the next ante is not a reason to spend the bank now. Spend only the surplus above $25 ($28 with a rental), and put it into targeted buys (main-hand planets, the xMult), not into chains of rerolls.",
   "evidence": "Decisions 20-22: three rerolls ($18) at 100% safety found nothing. Ante 3, decisions 24-36: from $34 to $9 while the next blind sat at 99-100% and the boss at 60-85%. Ante 4, decisions 43-58: from $17 to $0 while the boss was 85-95% safe. Every one of these was justified by a weak outlook for the next ante, not by the blind in front of it.",
   "cost": "Lost $3-5 of interest every round from ante 3 onward, and it left no money for the planets and xMult that ante 5 needed. The run ended with no buying power."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Before a boss that costs money (The Tooth: $1 per card played), or with rentals active, enter with at least (cards you expect to play + $3 per rental + $5) in cash. Never spend to $0 the shop before such a boss when it is already 85%+ safe.",
   "evidence": "At decision 58 the run spent its last $3 (Shoot the Moon -> Mystic Summit [eternal]) to lift The Tooth from 87% to 96%, then went in at $0 with a rental. It came out at -$12. At decisions 60-64 it could not buy anything at ante 5, and the outlook was 38% / 17% / 0%.",
   "cost": "Gave up the whole ante 5 shop phase, the last chance to fix scoring. A 9-point gain on an already-safe boss cost the run."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Do not sell a permanent x3 xMult to fix a single boss. Change how you play to fit it (for Flower Pot, play Two Pair or five-card hands that contain all four suits). Only swap it out if the replacement improves the outlook for the next ante, not just the current boss. Playbook rule 1 [measured]: one x3 joker is worth +2.5 blinds.",
   "evidence": "Decision 38 took Flower Pot (x3, no stickers) as the second xMult, ahead of the target. Decision 41 sold it for Mime to answer The Mouth, when the boss was already 85% with Flower Pot. Mime only pays with Kings in hand or steel cards held in hand, and the deck had 4 Kings and, by ante 5, 2 steel cards.",
   "cost": "Went from 2 xMult jokers to 1 conditional one. Ante 4 and ante 5 outlooks stayed at 0-15% for the boss from then on."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Pick one main hand by ante 2 and take every planet for it, even when the one-blind outlook rates another planet higher. Measure progress against the level targets: 2-3 by ante 2, 3-5 by antes 3-4, 6-9 by antes 5-6.",
   "evidence": "Decisions 19 and 25 took Uranus (Two Pair) over Mercury. Decision 25 said 'Mercury shows no measured gain', yet the plan said Pair was the default and the held-in-hand build wanted few played cards. Mercury only came at decision 53. At ante 5 the levels were Pair 2 and Two Pair 3, split across two hands.",
   "cost": "The main hand was about 4-6 levels under target at ante 5, which is about +1 blind of measured value lost. That is part of why the ante 5 Big Blind was only 17%."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "smoke2.jsonl#0#7000100",
 "summary": "The run got through antes 1-4 on cheap Pair jokers (Sly, Jolly), a holo Photograph, and a lucky Soul that gave Triboulet at ante 4. It never built around those two conditional xMult jokers. At ante 5 the deck still had 53 cards with only 4 Kings and 4 Queens, nothing was thinned, and Pair was level 4 against a target of 6-9. In ante 5 it spent $35 down to $10 on packs and a reroll. Two Standard Jumbos were opened and skipped, and the Celestial packs gave one Mercury. It then skipped the Big Blind for a Charm Tag and opened The Arm with a 3% clear chance, scoring about 3-6k per hand against 50k.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Once a rank-conditional xMult joker lands (Triboulet, Photograph, Scary Face-style face payoffs, Baron, etc.), every deck-editing consumable should raise the density of the trigger ranks. Use Strength (J→Q, Q→K), Death (copy a K/Q onto a low card) and Hanged Man (destroy 2s-7s). Prefer them in Arcana/Spectral picks over Wheel of Fortune, suit changers and enhancements on non-trigger ranks. Aim for at least 12 trigger cards, or remove 8+ low cards, before the next boss.",
   "evidence": "Triboulet arrived at #35 (ante 4). After that the Arcana picks were Wheel of Fortune (#37, over Moon/High Priestess), Justice on K♦ (#42), The Star used for a one-off flush (#43/#46), and Justice on a Jack that Triboulet ignores (#71). The ante 5 deck was 53 cards with 4 K and 4 Q, and the 3 2s, 5 3s and other low cards were never removed. The Arm round opened at 3% because the hand held no K/Q pair to trigger both xMults.",
   "cost": "The two xMult jokers fired in only a minority of hands. A board that cleared the 25k Small Blind could not reach 50k on the boss. This was the main reason the run lost."
  },
  {
   "rule": "From ante 5, when the next boss is under 50%, do not spend survival money on Standard packs hoping for specific ranks or on Celestial packs hoping for one planet. Weigh each $ against what it adds to the boss estimate. Prefer joker rerolls (any xMult or a big +Mult replacing a flat chips joker), a Pair planet sitting in the shop, or a tarot that directly edits trigger cards. If two buys in a row miss, stop and keep the interest.",
   "evidence": "#55 Standard Jumbo $6, skipped. #57 Celestial Mega $8, one Mercury plus Pluto. #63 Standard Jumbo $6, skipped. #65 reroll $5. #66 Celestial $4, Venus (off-plan). That was $29 for +1 Pair level. Money fell from $35 to $10 and the boss chance stayed around 41%.",
   "cost": "$29 spent and about $3/round of interest lost, with no gain in boss clear chance. There was no money left for a joker swap or rerolls before The Arm."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Treat the boss outlook as optimistic when the board's xMult depends on drawing specific ranks or when the boss drains levels (The Arm, Plasma-like penalties). Do not skip the last shop before such a boss for a random pack tag. Play the Big Blind if it is at least 60% and use that shop to fix the board. Skipping only removes risk if the boss estimate is trustworthy.",
   "evidence": "#69 skipped the Big Blind (65%) for a Charm Tag. The reasoning was that the boss at 43% was better than 0.67 × 0.5. The Mega Arcana gave Emperor and Justice (#70-71), and Judgement was useless with full joker slots. The Arm then opened at 3% (#72) and never went above 15%.",
   "cost": "Lost the $4 reward, about $2 interest and the only shop before the boss, all to buy into a boss estimate that was 40 points too high."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Main-hand levels are the second engine and the playbook targets (3-5 by ante 3-4, 6-9 by ante 5) must be met on time. From ante 3, buy every Mercury (or main-hand planet) seen in a shop. Prefer a Celestial pack to Arcana and Standard packs in any shop where the board is already safe. Value Telescope or Planet Merchant-type pickups highly when Pair is the plan.",
   "evidence": "Pair was still level 1 after 11 plays at ante 3 (#23). It was only level 3 entering ante 5 and 4 at the boss. In ante 4, with the board at 84-95%, money went to three Arcana packs (#34, #36, #41) and The Star (#43). The one Celestial pack bought (#49) missed.",
   "cost": "Every Pair hand at ante 5 scored about 2 levels below plan. The Arm's level drain then pushed it lower still, which is part of why the best hands reached only about 6k against a 50k boss."
  },
  {
   "rule": "In antes 1-2, do not buy an Eternal joker that will be a weak flat effect by ante 4 (e.g. Eternal Sly/Arrowhead) unless the next blind is below 70% without it. Do not spend $15+ rerolling a single shop. Spread rerolls, and stop when the next reroll would take you below $10.",
   "evidence": "#2 bought Eternal Sly. By ante 5 it was a permanently locked +50-chips slot that could not be sold for an upgrade (#68 found every swap negative, and Judgement had no room). #10-12 spent $18 on rerolls in one shop ($26→$8). Then #17 went to $0, and money stayed below $10 until the Handy Tag skip at ante 3.",
   "cost": "About $8-10 of lost interest across antes 2-3, and one of the five joker slots blocked for the whole run."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "smoke2.jsonl#1#7000101",
 "summary": "The run was stable through ante 5. Joker Stencil (made Negative by Ectoplasm), Square Joker and Two Pair levelled to 6 got it there, but it never found a second multiplier. It reached ante 6 scoring about 17k per hand against blinds that need 30k or more per hand. The deciding mistake was building around Stencil's empty slots: almost every joker was turned down because it would 'cost Stencil x1', including a free Rare joker. Money was then poured into rerolls that broke the playbook's limits, and both ante-7 blinds were skipped, so the run faced The Hook at 21% with no shop and died there.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Joker Stencil is a stopgap, not a plan. From ante 4, do not turn down a joker just because it lowers Stencil by x1. If it is an xMult joker, a scaling joker, or anything that grows (Bloodstone, Baron, Blackboard, Loyalty Card, any free Rare), take it whenever the board is below 2 xMult jokers, and judge it by the score needed 1-2 antes ahead, not by the next blind's clear chance. Empty slots never grow. A Stencil at x2-x3 is worth less than a second real multiplier by ante 6.",
   "evidence": "Jokers were turned down mainly to protect Stencil at 61 (Bloodstone), 68 (pack skipped), 73/77/81 (Jolly etc.), 89/91/93 (Blackboard), 97-100 (Loyalty Card), 112, 115 (Supernova), 133 (Baron, very likely the free Rare from the tag taken at 128) and 137. The board stayed at 3-5 jokers with one xMult. Best hand was about 17k at ante 6 and 28.6k at ante 7, against roughly 30k and 55k per hand needed.",
   "cost": "The run missed the playbook target of 2 xMult jokers by ante 7. Ante 6's Needle was 28% before the glass fix, ante 7's Hook was 14-21%, and the run died in ante 7."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Keep to the reroll limits even when a boss looks bad. Do at most 2-3 rerolls per shop. Never pay $9-10 for a reroll unless you hold $60 or more. Once one shop has used about $20 on rerolls, stop and save the rest for the next shop. If the boss itself is the problem, look first for a fix that targets that boss: a Director's Cut, glass from Justice, or a skip that avoids the boss.",
   "evidence": "In the ante-6 shop (103-114) the strategist rerolled six times at $5/6/7/8/9/10 and opened two Arcana Jumbos, going from $67 to $25. The $10 reroll at 114 was made at $35. All of that bought Hanging Chad ($1), Odd Todd (perishable) and some tarots. The Needle was actually cleared with two Justice cards (129-131), which cost nothing in that shop. At ante 7, rerolls 135-136 took money from $21 to $10.",
   "cost": "About $45 of rerolls bought no multiplier, and the run lost $3-4 of interest per round for the last two antes. It reached the ante-7 boss with nothing it could afford."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Do not skip blinds in an ante where the boss is below about 40% unless the tag pays off before the boss. A Rare, Uncommon or Voucher tag appears in the shop after the boss, so skipping both blinds sends you into the boss with exactly the same board. If the board is too weak for the blinds, the shops between them are the only way to raise boss odds. And when a Rare Tag's free joker shows up, take it, selling a perishable or flat joker to make room.",
   "evidence": "At 128 the strategist skipped the ante-6 Big Blind for a Rare Tag, so it met the Needle with no shop. At 133 it apparently turned down that free Rare (Baron) to protect Stencil. At 138-139 it skipped both ante-7 blinds (Speed Tag, then another Rare Tag that could only arrive after the Hook), and went into the Hook at 21% holding $25 it could not spend.",
   "cost": "It faced the deciding boss with the same weak board and money it could not spend. The second Rare Tag was never used."
  },
  {
   "rule": "In antes 3-4, while the board has only one multiplier, spend the surplus above $25 on Buffoon packs and on shop jokers, not on Spectral, Arcana or Standard packs that add one-card tweaks. Open a non-Buffoon pack only when you are aiming at a specific card the build needs (Uranus for the main hand, Justice for a Hanging Chad plan).",
   "evidence": "At 31-34 a Spectral Jumbo gave Aura and an Arcana Jumbo gave The Devil (one gold card). At 48-49 a Standard pack gave a purple-seal Ace. At 73-76 Arcana packs gave The Hermit and The Empress (two Mult cards). That is about $30 for minor deck tweaks while the board had a single xMult. Meanwhile Buffoon pack picks at 68 were skipped because of Stencil.",
   "cost": "About $30 of early surplus went to cards that added little scoring, instead of jokers that could have found the second multiplier while money was plentiful ($39-56)."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "teacher_v2.jsonl#8#7010008",
 "summary": "The run ended in Ante 1 without beating a single blind. It lost the 300-chip Small Blind with no jokers. The log has only one decision (#1, playing the Small Blind instead of skipping), and that choice was correct. None of the hand or discard decisions were recorded, so it can't be said exactly which play lost. Still, 300 chips over 4 hands with no jokers and every hand at level 1 is only lost one way: by playing low hands like Pair and High Card. At level 1, a Pair scores about 50-65 and High Card about 15-25, and four of those can't reach 300. The playbook's 'Default: Pair' rule seems to have been followed in a spot where it doesn't work.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Before the first joker (Ante 1, no scoring jokers), do not use Pair as the default hand. The Small Blind needs about 75 chips per hand over 4 hands. A level-1 Pair scores about 50-65, so play and discard toward Flush (about 280+ alone), Straight (about 200+), Three of a Kind (about 160-180) or Two Pair (about 100-120). Only fall back to Pair once a +Mult or chips joker is on the board.",
   "evidence": "Decision 1 played the Small Blind with no jokers, and the plan note said 'Default main hand is Pair.' The run then lost a 300-chip blind with 4 hands and 2 discards, which only happens if the scoring hands averaged under 75. That points to pairs and high cards being played.",
   "cost": "The whole run. It died on the first blind with 0 blinds beaten. The playbook's Pair default assumes jokers are already supplying mult, which isn't true in the first round."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Before every hand, compute chips still needed ÷ hands left. If your best available hand doesn't reach that number, spend a discard to chase a better hand (keep 4 of a suit for a Flush, keep a pair or trips for Three or Four of a Kind) instead of playing a weak hand. On the last hand, play only a hand that clears the remainder, or the single highest-scoring option.",
   "evidence": "No play or discard decisions were recorded after decision 1, but the blind was lost. With only 2 discards at Gold, every discard has to go toward reaching the per-hand target. Playing a weak hand early leaves later hands needing more than any level-1 hand can score.",
   "cost": "Losing the Small Blind also cost the first shop, so no joker was ever bought."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Log every play and discard as a numbered decision, with the chips needed, the expected score of the chosen hand, and the best alternative. A run that ends with no play decisions in its log should be treated as a bug in how hands are played or recorded, not as bad luck.",
   "evidence": "The log jumps from decision 1 (blind_select) straight to 'lost, died in ante 1.' Nothing shows how the 4 hands and 2 discards were used, and the reason text for decision 1 is cut off.",
   "cost": "The cause of the loss can't be pinned down, so the hand-play mistake that ended this run can't be confirmed or fixed for future runs."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "teacher_v2.jsonl#21#7010021",
 "summary": "The run died on its first blind: the ante 1 Small Blind (300 chips) with no jokers and every hand at level 1. Only one decision was logged (#1, play the Small Blind). That choice was correct. The loss came in the hand-by-hand play that followed, and those plays were not recorded. The plan note going into the round said 'Default main hand is Pair' and 'lean on non-spade pairs'. With no jokers, a level-1 Pair scores about 44-64 chips (Pair of Aces: (10+22)x2 = 64). That is below the 75 per hand needed to make 300 in 4 hands. So the playbook's Pair default, applied to a board with no jokers, could not win this blind even if every hand drew well.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Before playing any blind, divide the target by the hands left to get a per-hand score, and compare it with what your chosen hand type actually scores on the current board. With no jokers at ante 1 (300 needs 75 per hand), a level-1 Pair (at most about 64) cannot get there. Play the strongest hand the cards allow (Flush about 180+, Straight about 160+, Three of a Kind about 90-120, Two Pair about 60-80) and spend both discards digging for Flush, Straight or Trips. Do not discard toward a Pair.",
   "evidence": "Decision 1 went into the Small Blind with the plan 'Default main hand is Pair'. The result was 0 blinds beaten, a loss at 300 chips with no jokers. Four Pairs at level 1 total about 180-256, which is short of 300 even with perfect draws.",
   "cost": "The whole run. It ended on the first blind, before the first shop."
  },
  {
   "rule": "The playbook's 'Default: Pair' is a rule for a built board (+Mult or chips jokers in place). It is not a rule for a hand with no jokers. Until you own at least one scoring joker or a planet for Pair, choose each hand by the highest score it makes now, not by your long-term main hand.",
   "evidence": "The plan note at decision 1 tied hand choice to the long-term Pair plan while the board had no jokers and all hands were level 1. Here the playbook's advice was wrong for the situation: its Pair default assumes the 'cheap +Mult by ante 1-2' it recommends in the same section.",
   "cost": "It turned an easy blind into a loss (300 in 4 hands is usually cleared with a Flush or Straight plus one more hand)."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Keep boss-specific restrictions out of the earlier blinds. A boss debuff such as Goad (Spades debuffed) applies only to the boss blind. In the Small and Big Blinds, play and keep Spades normally. Also, fill in the outlook (per-hand target vs. expected score) before every blind. If it reads 'safe through -' with nothing computed, the risk check was skipped.",
   "evidence": "The decision 1 note said 'lean on non-spade pairs' for a Goad boss two blinds away. The outlook field at decision 1 was empty ('next , boss , safe through -'), so no score check was done before the blind that lost the run.",
   "cost": "Possibly cut down usable cards (Spade flushes and pairs) in the losing blind. With no outlook, nothing flagged that the planned hand type was below the needed score."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "teacher_v2.jsonl#13#7010013",
 "summary": "The run died to the first boss, The Manacle (600 chips, hand size cut to 7). It beat the Small and Big Blinds with almost no scoring, then went into the boss with only Splash, which adds a few chips, and a rental Droll Joker that only works on Flushes. It still had $22 unspent. The outlook gave 87-93% clear chances for a board with no +Mult that works on ordinary hands, and the strategist treated those numbers as permission to save money instead of buying scoring. What lost the run was banking money at ante 1 while it owned no real scoring joker.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "In antes 1-2, until you own at least one joker that adds Mult (or xMult) on your main hand, do not leave a shop holding more than about $8-10. Reroll or buy until such a joker appears. 'Survival overrides interest' outranks 'don't reroll below $20-25' and 'never drop an interest tier' when the board has no real scoring.",
   "evidence": "#4: left the shop at $15 with only Splash because the two jokers on offer were rentals and the outlook said 87%. #7: left at $22 with Splash plus a Flush-only Droll. A $5 reroll at either shop would have kept enough money to stay solvent. The run died to the Manacle with about $22 banked, so the $2-4 of interest it protected was never paid.",
   "cost": "The whole run. It died in ante 1 holding enough money for 3-4 rerolls."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Do not trust a high clear chance from the outlook when you have no unconditional +Mult. Check it yourself: a level-1 Pair scores about 60-110 chips, so 4 hands make roughly 250-450. That is below a 600 boss unless you land a Flush or three of a kind. If your own estimate of your main hand x 4 hands is below the blind, treat the blind as at risk, whatever percentage the outlook shows.",
   "evidence": "#4 (87%) and #6/#7 (93%) estimated the Manacle boss with Splash and Droll. Against a Pair plan, Droll adds nothing. Under Manacle you hold 7 cards and have 2 discards, so Flushes rarely land. The boss was lost.",
   "cost": "It cost the run. The strategist read a 7-13% risk as small and stopped spending."
  },
  {
   "rule": "A survival bridge joker must work on the hand you actually plan to play. Do not buy a hand-type-specific joker (Droll/Crafty for Flush, Crazy for Straight) as the answer to a boss while your declared main hand is Pair. Buy unconditional +Mult or chips (Jolly, Half, Abstract, Banner, Blackboard, Popcorn), or reroll to find one.",
   "evidence": "#6 bought a rental Droll (Flush +10 Mult) as the 'Manacle bridge', yet the plan note says 'Default main hand is Pair.' The extra 6% it was credited with depended on drawing Flushes with a 7-card hand and 2 discards.",
   "cost": "$1 plus $3 rent, and its slot and price stood in for a joker that would have helped every hand. That was the main reason the boss failed."
  },
  {
   "rule": "When opening a Buffoon pack with no jokers, rank the choices by how many chips they add to an ordinary Pair right now. Splash and other utility jokers are close to zero at ante 1. Locking one in as Eternal is only acceptable if the rest of the pack is worse and the next shop is still used to buy real Mult.",
   "evidence": "#3 took an Eternal Splash, which adds only the chip values of the non-scoring cards, about +15-25 per hand. The plan treated it as 'scoring help', and that made the next two shops (#4, #7) feel safe enough to skip spending.",
   "cost": "The $4 pack bought almost no scoring, and the strategist overrated how much the board could score."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "teacher_v2.jsonl#11#7010011",
 "summary": "The run got a free Card Sharp (x3) at ante 1 and cleared ante 1 with it alone. It then reached ante 2 with nothing for Card Sharp to multiply: Pair at level 1, no planets, no flat chips, and one +Mult joker (Wrathful) that only works on spades. It died on the 1,000-chip ante 2 Small Blind, a blind the outlook had called a 100% clear. The loss came from trusting inflated clear estimates and holding money for interest ($18-19) when it should have bought base chips and mult. Bad luck was not the cause.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Before trusting a clear chance, check it with quick hand arithmetic: (base chips + card chips) x (mult) x (xMult), counting only the hands where each joker actually fires. If one cheap conditional joker (suit-only, hand-only) moves the outlook by more than ~30 points, or the arithmetic for a typical hand comes in under blind/3 per hand, treat the blind as unsafe and keep buying scoring.",
   "evidence": "At #10, buying Wrathful Joker (+3 Mult per scored spade only) was said to raise the Wheel boss from 21% to 88%, and #13 called the 1,000 Small Blind a 100% clear. The real numbers: a level-1 Pair of non-spades is about (10+20)x2 = 60, or 180 with Card Sharp. A spade pair is about (10+22)x8x3 = 770. Card Sharp does not fire on the first hand of a round. The board reached 1,000 in 4 hands only when it drew spade pairs, and it lost.",
   "cost": "Lost the run at the first ante 2 blind. The false 'safe' reading caused every pass in the shop."
  },
  {
   "rule": "An xMult joker with no base under it is not a build. Once you hold an early xMult, the next purchases must be an unconditional chips or +Mult joker and a planet for the main hand, before any interest target. By the ante 2 shop, have at least one unconditional base joker and Pair at level 2 or higher, even if that drops money to ~$10.",
   "evidence": "#3 took Card Sharp. #4 ($13) and #7 ($19) left the shop. #11 left at $18 and passed on both Jumbo packs and Grabber (+1 hand, which is one more Card Sharp repeat) to protect interest. Pair was still level 1 at ante 2 (playbook target: level 2-3).",
   "cost": "Saved maybe $1-2 of interest. Lost the run."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Exception to 'no rentals at ante 1' on Yellow Deck: if you hold an xMult joker and have no base, take a big flat-chips rental (Stuntman +250) or an affordable Bootstraps. The playbook's own Yellow Deck note says one rental is easy to carry, and Stuntman's chips are exactly what the xMult needs (Stuntman -> High Card/Pair is a listed commit signal).",
   "evidence": "At #2/#4, Stuntman was rejected as an ante 1 rental and Bootstraps as too expensive at $7. With Card Sharp, Stuntman makes a repeated Pair about (260+20)x2x3 = 1,680, enough to clear every ante 2 blind. The $3/round rent is far less than dying.",
   "cost": "Passed up the cheapest fix for the build's one weakness."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Survival overrides interest, and so does 'safe' reasoning based on a weak estimate. At antes 1-2, do not leave a shop holding $15+ while the board has fewer than 2 unconditional scoring sources. Spend to the next interest tier below and buy scoring.",
   "evidence": "#7 left with $19 and #11 left with $18 for interest. The playbook's 'Money is useless if you die' and 'One scoring joker by ante 2, about 2-3 by ante 3' were both broken in substance, because Wrathful's spade-only mult does not count as reliable scoring.",
   "cost": "The run ended in ante 2 with about $18 unspent."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "teacher_v2.jsonl#39#7010039",
 "summary": "The run ended at the ante-2 Big Blind (1,500 chips) after only 4 blinds, even though the outlook gave that blind 93% and the strategist had 5 hands. By then the board was Walkie Talkie (an eternal that only scores on 10s and 4s) and Flower Pot (x3 only when the scoring cards cover all four suits). Every scoring joker on the board needed a condition to work, and Two Pair was still level 1. The strategist spent $10 on Grabber and kept about $18-21 for interest instead of buying a reliable +Mult/chips source. It was relying on Flower Pot firing, and Flower Pot rarely fires on Two Pair.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Before ante 3, do not count a conditional joker (Flower Pot, Walkie Talkie, suit or rank jokers) as your scoring base. If you have no unconditional +Mult or chips joker and your main hand is level 1, buy one: a Buffoon pack, a reroll, or a planet for the hand you play. Do this before buying a voucher or holding money for interest, even when the outlook shows 90% or more.",
   "evidence": "At #7, #9 and #14 the strategist left the shop holding $17-21, citing 93-96% outlooks. The board at #15 was Walkie Talkie plus Flower Pot, with Two Pair at level 1 and no flat +Mult. It lost the 1,500-chip Big Blind. The playbook's 'one scoring joker by ante 2' meant a joker that scores on ordinary hands. A narrow eternal plus a conditional xMult does not meet that.",
   "cost": "The run: it died at ante 2 with $21 unspent."
  },
  {
