{
 "game": "teacher_v2.jsonl#23#7010023",
 "summary": "The run died to The Needle (2,000 chips in a single hand) at the ante 2 boss, after beating only 5 blinds. It went through ante 1 with Ice Cream as its only joker, a chips joker that shrinks each hand. Twice it turned down cheap +Mult to protect interest. Its one real Mult source, Gros Michel, was gone before the boss shop. The boss was known from the start of ante 2, but money went to things that don't help in one hand (Loyalty Card, Mercury, a $5 reroll). That left a last-shop scramble: $33 went down to $1 on rising rerolls and two Straight jokers with nothing to help draw a Straight, and the run went into the boss at 11%.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Once the ante's boss is shown, score the build against that boss's actual rule in every shop of the ante, not against the Small or Big Blind. For a one-hand boss (The Needle, or The Wall/Vessel later), only per-hand +Mult, xMult and planets for the main hand count. Do not buy delayed or counter-based jokers (Loyalty Card, Ride the Bus, Runner, Wee, Lucky Cat) or economy jokers until the one-hand score clears the boss.",
   "evidence": "#9 already showed The Needle at 47%. #16 bought an eternal Loyalty Card (X4 every 6 hands) for $5, calling the next blind safe, but it can never fire in a one-hand round. #20 then gave The Needle 11%, with the agent noting 'Loyalty won't trigger with only one hand.'",
   "cost": "$5, plus a permanent joker slot, spent on a card worth nothing against the boss that ended the run. That $5 could have been an early reroll or a pack while shop prices were still low."
  },
  {
   "rule": "In antes 1-2 the $25 hold does not apply while the next boss is below about 80%. Buy a cheap +Mult joker, even a perishable one (Raised Fist, Gros Michel, Jolly), when it raises the boss chance, even if it drops an interest tier. Reroll in the early shops of the ante rather than saving it all for the boss shop, where rerolls cost $6-7.",
   "evidence": "#4 and #7 left ante 1 shops with $15 and $24 and no +Mult. #7 turned down Raised Fist, a perishable that would have lasted through The Needle, to save $1 of interest a round. #12 left at $26 with The Needle at 64% and would not spend $6 on a reroll. The playbook says to reach $25 over antes 2-4 and hold it from ante 3, and that survival overrides interest.",
   "cost": "The run entered the boss shop with one fading chips joker and no lasting Mult. It then spent $18 on rerolls at higher prices ($5/$6/$7) in a single shop, plus $14 on cards that don't fit the build, and finished at $1 anyway."
  },
  {
   "rule": "When survival depends on one fragile joker (Gros Michel's 1-in-6 destruction, Ice Cream or Popcorn decay, perishable expiry), lower the clear chance by its failure odds. Keep buying Mult as backup until the boss clears even without that joker.",
   "evidence": "#15 bought Gros Michel as the only +Mult, and #17 then judged The Needle '96% safe' and left with $22. By #20 the best hand was about 312, which means Gros Michel was already gone, and The Needle stood at 11%.",
   "cost": "The 96% outlook relied on a single 5-in-6 joker. Once it broke, The Needle dropped to 11% with only one shop left to fix it."
  },
  {
   "rule": "In a desperate last shop, do not switch to a hand you can't reliably draw unless you count the odds of drawing it. With 8 cards, 2 discards and no Four Fingers or Shortcut, a Straight in one hand is roughly a 25-30% event. Prefer cards that raise the score of whatever you are likely to hold (flat Mult or chips on Pair or High Card), and keep enough money to sell and rebuild if something better shows up.",
   "evidence": "#24-25 spent the last $8 on Crazy Joker (perishable) and Devious Joker to make a Straight score 2,000+. The playbook says Straights need an enabler on Gold. #26 claimed 47%, but the round at #27 opened at 11% because the Straight never came.",
   "cost": "The last $8, spent on a plan that needed a draw the deck was unlikely to give. The run ended at ante 2."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "teacher_v2.jsonl#34#7010034",
 "summary": "The run cleared antes 1-3 with a weak board: a holo rental Even Steven, Walkie Talkie, Splash, and Ancient Joker as the only multiplier (x1.5, on one suit that changes every round). It then lost the ante 4 Small Blind (9,000), which the outlook had put at 93%. The planets went to Full House and Three of a Kind, both hard to draw, so Pair stayed at level 1. The strategist also left five shops in antes 2-4 holding $25-33 and never rerolled once. When the ante 3→4 jump (x2.8) came, there was no second multiplier and no levelled hand that showed up reliably.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Choose the main hand by how often you can draw it with 2 discards, not by how often you have played it so far. On a Gold-stake Pair-type build, Pair (or Two Pair or Three of a Kind with an enabler) is the main hand. Take Mercury whenever it is offered and points at no other committed hand. Do not make Full House the main hand unless you have a real enabler (heavy deck changes, or a joker that pays for Full House). Before calling a hand the main hand, check that you could draw it in 3 of 4 hands.",
   "evidence": "Decision 15 took Venus (Three of a Kind) as a 'free level'. Decision 21 passed on Mercury because 'Pair is not our main hand'. Decisions 24 and 27 put both Earths into Full House because it had been 'played 5 times'. At ante 4, Pair was still level 1, Three of a Kind level 2, Full House level 3, and every level spent was on a hand that only shows up some of the time. The playbook says 'Default: Pair' and 'plan one very good hand, not two'.",
   "cost": "Three planets went into hands the deck rarely makes, and the hand the deck does make was never levelled. Scoring at ante 4 rested on drawing Full House, and the run died on the first ante 4 blind."
  },
  {
   "rule": "From ante 2 on, if the board has fewer than 2 multiplier sources (counting x1.5 suit-dependent jokers like Ancient as half of one), spend the money above $25 in every shop. Use one or two $5 rerolls, or buy a +Mult or xMult joker, and do not walk out holding $27-33. Never pass a real xMult joker (The Idol, Gros Michel at +15) only to keep an interest tier when the build still has an open slot.",
   "evidence": "The strategist left the shop with money to spare at decisions 9 ($22), 11 ($25, passed Gros Michel), 19 ($22, passed The Idol because it 'would drop two interest tiers'), 21 ($25), 25 ($27) and 28 ($29). It did not reroll once in the whole run, and the fifth joker slot stayed empty from ante 3 until death. The playbook says to 'use surplus money for rerolls and packs to find xMult' in antes 3-5, and that 'no rerolls for 2-3 antes means the run is in trouble'.",
   "cost": "It died with about $29 banked, an empty joker slot, and only one weak multiplier. That unspent money could have paid for 4-5 rerolls or one xMult/+15 Mult joker going into ante 4."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Do not buy a rental joker in ante 1, even at $1, unless the outlook says the current blind will fail without it. If you do carry one, sell it once a non-rental scorer can replace it, and stop letting the $28 target block buys that improve the build.",
   "evidence": "Decision 2 quoted the rule 'no rentals at ante 1'. Decision 4 then bought Even Steven [rental] for $1. It cost $3 every round for about 11 rounds, and the bank target went up to $28 (decisions 22, 26, 28). Decision 28 then turned down shop items because they would 'take me under the $28 I need to hold with the rental'.",
   "cost": "About $30+ in rent over the run, plus the higher target, which blocked buys at the ante 4 shop just before the loss. That is money that could have paid for the missing xMult."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Do not trust a 90%+ outlook as permission to save money when it rests on situational pieces: Splash plus kickers, a suit-dependent multiplier (Ancient), or levels on hands that only show up some of the time. Before each ante step (especially ante 3→4, which is x2.8), check the score you can realistically repeat with an ordinary Pair or Two Pair hand against the next ante's Small Blind. If 4 such hands don't reach it, buy power now.",
   "evidence": "The outlook for The Wall swung from 40% (decision 17) to 91% after one Splash pick (decision 19). It showed 96% for nearly every blind through ante 3, and 93% for the ante 4 Small Blind (decision 29), which the run then lost. The 69-70% figure for The Goad (decisions 26, 29) already showed the build was close to its limit, but the response was only one $4 Celestial pack.",
   "cost": "Overconfident outlooks justified saving money in five shops. The run reached a 9,000-chip blind with a board that could not reliably score it, and lost at ante 4."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "teacher_v2.jsonl#18#7010018",
 "summary": "The run died at the ante 3 boss, The Mark. At the start of ante 3 the board was weak: two conditional x2 jokers (Seeing Double needs a scoring Club, and The Duo is perishable), Lusty, a 0-to-8-chip Square Joker, and Cloud 9 taking a slot. Two Pair was only level 3. The boss outlook swung from 47% to 91% across a few shops, and at decision 32 the strategist trusted the 91% to skip the Big Blind for an Economy Tag. That gave up the last shop before the boss. The round player then put the boss at 80% and played it with a grow-the-joker style, and the run lost there holding $50 it could no longer spend on that fight.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Do not skip the blind just before a boss (usually the Big Blind) for a money tag (Economy, Handy, Investment) unless the boss estimate has been at least 90% for two or more shops in a row and there is nothing you would want to buy in the shop you give up. Money from the tag cannot be spent before the boss, so it does nothing for survival.",
   "evidence": "Decision 32 skipped the Big Blind for the Economy Tag ($25 to $50) because of one 91% boss reading. The earlier readings for the same boss were 48% (#20), 47% (#21), 63% (#23-25) and 72% (#27). When the boss came, the round player put it at 80% (#33), and the run died holding $50 with no shop left to turn it into scoring.",
   "cost": "The run. The skip gave up one shop, Square Joker growth and the Big Blind payout, and the $25 gained could not help against the boss that ended the run."
  },
  {
   "rule": "When the boss clear estimate for the same boss swings by more than 20 points between shops with only small board changes, treat the lowest recent number as the real one. Keep buying scoring (a permanent +Mult/xMult joker, or a planet for the main hand) until the estimate stays at 90% or higher.",
   "evidence": "The Mark was rated 47-48% (#20-21), then 63%, 72% and 91% (#23, #27, #31) after only one Uranus and a Sun card. The strategist planned around the highest number (#31-32) instead of the earlier ones, and the round itself was rated 80% (#33).",
   "cost": "The strategist believed the run was safe when it wasn't, and this led straight to the skip and the loss."
  },
  {
   "rule": "On a boss blind rated below about 95%, the round style must maximize score this hand. Drop grow_joker and similar build-growth goals. Grow scaling jokers only on blinds that are about 100% safe.",
   "evidence": "Decision 33 played the deciding ante 3 boss at 80% with style grow_joker 2 (4-card Two Pair to feed Square Joker, worth only a few chips) instead of playing for maximum score each hand. Under The Mark, face cards are drawn face down, which makes it harder to land the scoring Club that Seeing Double needs.",
   "cost": "It gave up score on the hand that decided the run in exchange for future value the run never got to use."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Through ante 3, when the next boss is under 75%, fill joker slots with scoring rather than economy. Pick a value generator only when the next boss is already safe and a slot is free. Prefer Pair as the main hand over Two Pair, as the playbook says, and avoid building around conditional xMult (suit-gated or perishable) as your only multiplier.",
   "evidence": "At #17 the strategist took Cloud 9 over Walkie Talkie, and it then held a slot through an ante 3 where the boss sat at 47-72%. Decision 24 noted that Cloud 9 was a cost against adding scoring. The build committed to Two Pair (#12, #15), against the playbook's 'prefer Pair', and leaned on Seeing Double (needs a Club) plus a perishable Duo. Square Joker was still at +8 chips in ante 3.",
   "cost": "The board stayed under-powered for ante 3 (6,400-chip boss with no permanent xMult), so the run depended on a single boss roll."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "teacher_v2.jsonl#35#7010035",
 "summary": "The run died to The Mouth (6,400) in ante 3, with 8 blinds beaten. The outlook had rated that boss 94% safe all ante. The board was flat +Mult (Jolly, Smiley), a chips joker about to expire (Odd Todd), a perishable conditional x2 (Seeing Double) and an economy joker (Golden). Pair was only level 3, and the deck was cluttered with 6 Stone cards. The strategist's own number showed a thin margin: best hand about 1.6k against 1,280 needed per hand. Even so, it spent its ante-3 surplus on packs, rerolls and Golden Joker instead of scoring, and it passed on the one scoring joker offered by misreading a playbook rule.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Before you trust a high clear chance for a boss, check it yourself. Compare your typical hand (not your best hand) with the boss target divided by hands. If your typical hand is below about 1.5x that number, or a joker's condition (a suit requirement, The Mouth's hand lock, a card you hold back) is needed to reach it, treat the boss as unsafe. Do not buy economy jokers or open speculative packs that shop.",
   "evidence": "At #27 the strategist wrote that its best hand was about 1.6k. The Mouth needed 6,400 in 5 hands, about 1,280 per hand, and an ordinary pair of non-face even cards scores roughly 700. Yet #29-#35 kept quoting 94% and treated the boss as safe. The run then died on that boss.",
   "cost": "Lost the run at ante 3, blind 9."
  },
  {
   "rule": "In ante 3, when the board is only flat +Mult and has no xMult that lasts, spend the surplus above $25 on scoring first. That means a scoring joker, a main-hand planet, or a reroll that looks for one. Only after that consider Golden Joker or other money makers, or Arcana and Standard packs. A value generator needs the next boss to be clearly safe, not safe on paper.",
   "evidence": "In ante 3 the strategist spent about $27 on things that added no score at The Mouth. It bought an Arcana pack and took The Devil, which was never used (#20-21). It opened two Standard packs (#24, #31), which gave a glass 5 and a Stone blue-seal 10. It bought Golden Joker for $6 (#34) as the board's Odd Todd was about to expire.",
   "cost": "No extra scoring for the boss that killed the run. About $27 of a $39 peak bought nothing that helped survive."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Rentals are only banned at ante 1. From ante 3, a rental scoring joker that fits the main hand is a fine buy if you keep $28 banked, especially when your scoring jokers are perishable or about to expire. Do not turn the playbook's ante-1 rental warning into a rule for the whole run.",
   "evidence": "At #29 the strategist turned down Even Steven, which gives +4 Mult per even card, about +8 on even pairs, and the deck had 9 twos. Its reason was 'Even Steven is a rental at ante 3, which the playbook rules out.' The playbook only lists 'bought rentals at ante 1' as a failure and gives a $28 hold rule for carrying one rental. Two turns earlier it had bought a perishable x2 and kept a perishable chips joker.",
   "cost": "Turned down the only scoring joker on offer. Its main chips source (Odd Todd) was in its last round."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Do not take jokers that add non-pairing cards to a Pair deck (Marble Joker, which adds Stone cards). Do not plan to hold a Stone or blue-seal card in hand through a dangerous boss. Each dead card in hand cuts an 8-card hand to 7 live cards and makes pairs harder to draw.",
   "evidence": "At #5 the strategist took Marble Joker even though it scores nothing on its own. By ante 3 the deck had 5-6 Stone cards (#28 sold it, too late). At #32 it added a blue-seal Stone 10 and planned to keep it in hand every round. That shrank the usable hand during The Mouth, where only Pair counted and there were 2 discards.",
   "cost": "Lower odds of drawing pairs, especially pairs with a Club for Seeing Double, in the deciding boss fight. One Buffoon pick wasted at ante 1."
  },
  {
   "rule": "Do not build a scoring plan on perishable jokers. Count a perishable as gone at its expiry date, and from 2 rounds before that, look for a permanent replacement.",
   "evidence": "At #11 Odd Todd (perishable) was the run's only chips joker, and it expired at The Mouth. At #27 Seeing Double (perishable, and it needs a Club in the scoring hand) became the only xMult. At #35 the board leaned on both for the boss.",
   "cost": "The board had no lasting scaling, and its damage depended on drawing the right cards against a 6,400 boss."
  }
 ]
}
{
 "game": "teacher_v2.jsonl#30#7010030",
 "summary": "The run died at the ante 3 boss, The Fish (6,400). The board was Ice Cream (chips run down every hand), Misprint (random Mult), Gros Michel (can be destroyed), Mail-In Rebate (money only) and Splash. It had no multiplier and nothing that grows. Pair was levelled well, to level 4. But every scoring source was temporary or random, so the high clear chances (88-96%) fell apart: 92% before the boss at decision 39, 3% inside it at decision 40. The strategist kept passing on lasting power (Hanging Chad, a growing joker, packs) to stay at $25. It was sitting at $24-38 the whole time, so the money was there to buy it.",
 "lessons": [
  {
   "rule": "Count how much of the board's scoring is unreliable: jokers that shrink (Ice Cream, Popcorn, Seltzer), random ones (Misprint) and ones that can be destroyed (Gros Michel). If more than half of it is like that, do not leave a shop because the outlook says the boss is safe. Work out the bad case instead: Gros Michel gone, Misprint at about +5, the shrinking joker after 4 more hands. If that does not clear the boss in 4 hands, buy lasting scoring, even if it costs an interest tier.",
   "evidence": "Decision 29 passed on everything at 94%. Decision 33 passed on the Celestial Mega at 92%. Decision 39 left with $26 at 92%. Each time the reason given was 'survival is fine'. At decision 32 the best hand was about 1.4k, so 4 hands made about 5.6k against a 6,400 boss. The boss chance had already slid from 94% (decision 29) to 80% (decision 32) to 65% (decision 36) as Ice Cream lost chips. Inside the boss, at decision 40, it was 3%.",
   "cost": "This lost the run. The strategist had $23-38 through all of ante 3 and died at the boss holding $26."
